                "content": "**Notation**\n\n*   \\(A_{2}\\) \u2013 di\u2011functional \u201cA\u2019\u2019 monomer (two A\u2011type groups)  \n*   \\(B_{2}\\) \u2013 di\u2011functional \u201cB\u2019\u2019 monomer (two B\u2011type groups)  \n*   \\(B_{3}\\) \u2013 tri\u2011functional \u201cB\u2019\u2019 monomer (three B\u2011type groups)  \n\nAll functional groups of the same kind are chemically identical and react\nrandomly.  \nNo intramolecular cyclisations are allowed, therefore every reaction joins\ntwo different fragments and reduces the number of connected clusters by **one**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Mean number of *other* B groups on the same monomer  \n\nPick a B functional group at random.  \nThe probability that it belongs to a particular monomer type \\(i\\) is proportional\nto the number of B groups carried by that type:\n\n\\[\nP(\\hbox{type }i)=\\frac{N_i\\,f_i}{\\displaystyle\\sum_j N_j f_j},\n\\qquad \nf_i=\\hbox{functionality of type }i .\n\\]\n\nIf the chosen group sits on a monomer of type \\(i\\) there remain\n\\(f_i-1\\) *other* B groups on that monomer.  \nHence the required mean is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\ng_B=\\sum_i P(i)\\,(f_i-1)\n      =\\frac{\\displaystyle\\sum_i N_i f_i\\,(f_i-1)}\n             {\\displaystyle\\sum_i N_i f_i}\n     }                                   \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Evaluation for the mixture \\(B_{2}:B_{3}=9:1\\)\n\nTake \\(N_{B_2}=9k,\\;N_{B_3}=k\\;(k>0)\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_i N_i f_i &= 2(9k)+3(k)=18k+3k=21k ,\\\\[2mm]\n\\sum_i N_i f_i(f_i-1)&=2(9k)(1)+3(k)(2)=18k+6k=24k .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{g_B=\\frac{24k}{21k}= \\frac{8}{7}=1.142857\\ldots }\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Why the ordinary number\u2011average functionality is wrong  \n\nThe number\u2011average functionality would be  \n\n\\[\n\\bar f=\\frac{2(9)+3(1)}{9+1}= \\frac{21}{10}=2.1 .\n\\]\n\nThat weighting treats each *molecule* equally, whereas the experiment\nselects a **functional group**, i.e. a monomer bearing many groups is\nchosen preferentially.  Consequently the correct weighting is by the\nnumber of functional groups, which leads to Eq.(1) and the value\n\\(g_B=8/7\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Flory\u2013Stockmayer gel condition for an \\(A_{2}\\!-\\!B_f\\) system  \n\nIn the tree\u2011approximation a reaction creates a new \u201cbranch\u2019\u2019 that continues\nfrom the partner functional group.  The probability that a growing chain\ndoes **not** terminate (i.e. that it will eventually become part of the\ninfinite gel) is the branching factor  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda = p_A g_A = p_B g_B .\n\\]\n\nBecause the A\u2011type monomers are di\u2011functional, \\(g_A=1\\) (after one bond\nis made only one A group remains).  The gel point is reached when the\nmean number of descendants exceeds one:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p_A p_B g_B = 1 }                                            \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWith the step\u2011growth restriction \\(p_A=p_B\\equiv p\\),\n\n\\[\np_c = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{g_B}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{8}}\n        = 0.9354\\; (\\text{dimensionless}) .                         \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Stoichiometric amount of \\(A_{2}\\) and polymerisation at \\(p=0.900\\)\n\nNumber of B functional groups  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{B\u2011groups}} = 2(9.00)+3(1.00)=21.00\\;\\text{mol}.\n\\]\n\nTo have exact A/B balance we need the same number of A groups,\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{n_{A_2}= \\frac{21.00}{2}=10.50\\ \\text{mol}} .\n\\]\n\nInitial total number of monomer molecules  \n\n\\[\nN_0 = n_{A_2}+n_{B_2}+n_{B_3}=10.50+9.00+1.00=20.50\\ \\text{mol}.\n\\]\n\nEach reacted A\u2013B pair removes one connected cluster, therefore after a\nconversion \\(p\\) the number of clusters is  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{clusters}} = N_0 - p\\,N_{\\text{B\u2011groups}} .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(p=0.900\\)\n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{clusters}} = 20.50-0.900\\times 21.00 = 20.50-18.90 = 1.60 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the number\u2011average degree of polymerisation  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nX_n = \\frac{N_0}{N_{\\text{clusters}}}\n     = \\frac{20.50}{1.60}=12.8 } .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p=0.900 < p_c=0.935\\), the system is **below** the gel point,\nas expected from the finite \\(X_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Extinction\u2011probability equations for \\(p=0.960\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n*   \\(x_A\\) \u2013 probability that a chain started from a randomly chosen **A**\n    functional group **does not** reach the infinite gel (i.e. it terminates);\n*   \\(x_B\\) \u2013 analogous probability for a **B** functional group.\n\nWhen a functional group is examined three situations are possible:\n\n| event | probability | consequence |\n|-------|-------------|--------------|\n| the group stays unreacted | \\(1-p\\) | immediate termination \u21d2 extinction |\n| the group reacts (probability \\(p\\)) and the partner is a B\u2011group belonging to a \\(B_2\\) monomer | \\(\\displaystyle p\\frac{2N_{B_2}}{2N_{B_2}+3N_{B_3}}\\) | one remaining B\u2011group on that monomer \u2192 extinction probability \\(x_B\\) |\n| the group reacts (probability \\(p\\)) and the partner is a B\u2011group belonging to a \\(B_3\\) monomer | \\(\\displaystyle p\\frac{3N_{B_3}}{2N_{B_2}+3N_{B_3}}\\) | two remaining B\u2011groups \u2192 extinction probability \\(x_B^{2}\\) |\n\nUsing the composition \\(B_2:B_3=9:1\\) gives the fractions  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2N_{B_2}}{2N_{B_2}+3N_{B_3}}=\\frac{6}{7},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{3N_{B_3}}{2N_{B_2}+3N_{B_3}}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_A = (1-p) + p\\Bigl(\\frac{6}{7}x_B+\\frac{1}{7}x_B^{2}\\Bigr)} . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor a B functional group the situation is simpler: with probability\n\\(1-p\\) it stays free (termination) and with probability \\(p\\) it reacts\nto an A group, after which the chain continues from that A monomer.\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_B = (1-p) + p\\,x_A}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Solving for \\(p=0.960\\)\n\nInsert \\(p=0.960\\) :\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B &= 0.040 + 0.960\\,x_A ,\\\\[2mm]\nx_A &= 0.040 + 0.960\\Bigl(\\tfrac{6}{7}x_B+\\tfrac{1}{7}x_B^{2}\\Bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEliminating \\(x_B\\) yields a quadratic for \\(x_A\\):\n\n\\[\n0.126391\\,x_A^{2}-0.199355\\,x_A+0.073134=0 .\n\\]\n\nIts two roots are  \n\n\\[\nx_A^{(1)}\\approx 0.997\\quad\\text{and}\\quad x_A^{(2)}\\approx 0.580 .\n\\]\n\nThe physically relevant extinction probability must be **smaller** than\nthe unit solution (otherwise no finite clusters could exist).  \nTherefore we retain the **lower** root:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_A = 0.580\\;( \\text{\u2248 }5.8\\times10^{-1}) } ,\n\\qquad\n\\boxed{x_B = 0.040+0.960\\times0.580 = 0.597 } .\n\\]\n\nBoth are comfortably below 1, confirming that above the gel point a\nfinite probability of extinction still exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Fraction of monomer molecules that belong to the infinite gel  \n\nA monomer is **in the sol** (i.e. not in the infinite gel) only if **all**\nits branches terminate.  For the three monomer types:\n\n*   \\(A_2\\) : two A\u2011branches \u2192 sol probability \\(x_A^{2}\\);\n*   \\(B_2\\) : two B\u2011branches \u2192 sol probability \\(x_B^{2}\\);\n*   \\(B_3\\) : three B\u2011branches \u2192 sol probability \\(x_B^{3}\\).\n\nWith the numbers from part\u202f3 (\\(n_{A_2}=10.5,\\;n_{B_2}=9,\\;n_{B_3}=1\\))\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{sol molecules}&=\n10.5\\,x_A^{2}+9\\,x_B^{2}+1\\,x_B^{3}\\\\\n&=10.5(0.580)^{2}+9(0.597)^{2}+ (0.597)^{3}\\\\\n&=10.5(0.3364)+9(0.3564)+0.2128\\\\\n&=3.532+3.208+0.213\\approx 7.0\\;\\text{mol}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTotal monomer molecules = 20.5\u202fmol, thus the **fraction in the sol** is  \n\n\\[\nf_{\\text{sol}}=\\frac{7.0}{20.5}=0.34 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the **fraction belonging to the infinite gel** is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f_{\\text{gel}} = 1-f_{\\text{sol}}\\approx 0.66\\;(66\\%)} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Why the unit solution \\((x_A=x_B=1)\\) is not physical  \n\nEquation set (4)\u2013(5) possesses the trivial fixed point \\(x_A=x_B=1\\),\nwhich satisfies the algebraic relations for any \\(p\\).  It corresponds to\nthe limit in which **every** chain certainly reaches the infinite gel,\ni.e. the extinction probability is zero.  Such a situation would require\nthat the mean progeny of the branching process be **exactly** one at\nzero conversion, which is not the case here (the mean progeny is\n\\(g_B=8/7>1\\)).  \n\nIn a Galton\u2013Watson branching process the fixed points of the generating\nfunction are:\n\n*   \\(q=1\\) \u2013 always unstable (any fluctuation drives the process away);\n*   a second root \\(q<1\\) \u2013 **stable** when the mean progeny exceeds one.\n\nFor \\(p>p_c\\) the process is supercritical; a non\u2011zero probability of\nsurvival (joining the infinite gel) coexists with a non\u2011zero probability\nof extinction (remaining in the sol).  The **stable** root is the\nphysically realized extinction probability, while the unstable root\nrepresents the mathematical artefact that would correspond to a completely\nun\u2011gelled system (subcritical regime).  \n\nThus the non\u2011trivial solution obtained in part\u202f4 correctly describes the\nfraction of chains that die out, whereas the unit solution would predict\nthat **no** finite clusters exist\u2014contrary to observation.  The stability\nargument is precisely what underlies the Flory\u2013Stockmayer criterion\n\\(p_A p_B g_B =1\\); once the mean progeny exceeds unity, the branching\nprocess becomes supercritical and the extinction probability drops\nbelow one, as quantified by the equations solved above.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Summary of numerical results**\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|----------|-------|\n| \\(g_B\\) | \\(8/7 = 1.1429\\) |\n| Critical conversion \\(p_c\\) | \\(0.935\\) |\n| Required \\(A_2\\) for stoichiometry | \\(10.5\\)\u202fmol |\n| \\(X_n\\) at \\(p=0.900\\) | \\(12.8\\) |\n| Extinction probabilities at \\(p=0.960\\) | \\(x_A=0.580,\\;x_B=0.597\\) |\n| Fraction of monomers in infinite gel (at \\(p=0.960\\)) | \\(\\approx 0.66\\) |\n\nThese results illustrate how the simple \u201caverage functionality\u2019\u2019 is\ninsufficient, how the Flory\u2013Stockmayer gel condition emerges from a\nbranching\u2011process picture, and how the extinction\u2011probability formalism\nquantifies the size of the infinite network once the critical conversion\nhas been passed.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer multi-part question about step-growth polymerization with A2, B2, B3 monomers, ratio B2:B3 =9:1. Need to compute g_B (mean number of other B functional groups on same monomer when selecting a random B functional group). Use formula g_B = \u03a3 N_i f_i(f_i-1)/\u03a3 N_i f_i where N_i is number of monomers of type i? Actually we have two types of B monomers: B2 (functionality 2) and B3 (functionality 3). Let N2 = number of B2 molecules, N3 = number of B3 molecules. Then total B functional groups = 2N2 + 3N3. When picking a random B functional group, probability it's on a B2 monomer = 2N2/(2N2+3N3); similarly for B3 = 3N3/(total). Expected number of other B groups on that monomer = (f_i -1). So expectation = \u03a3 (probability)*(f_i-1) = (2N2/(tot))(1) + (3N3/(tot))(2) = (2N2 +6N3)/tot. But expression given uses \u03a3 N_i f_i(f_i-1)/\u03a3 N_i f_i. Let's check: For each monomer type i, contribution N_i f_i(f_i-1). For B2: N2 *2*1 =2N2. For B3: N3*3*2=6N3. Sum numerator =2N2+6N3. Denominator \u03a3 N_i f_i =2N2+3N3. Ratio = (2N2+6N3)/(2N2+3N3). That's same as earlier because dividing numerator and denominator by total B groups? Wait earlier we had numerator =2N2+6N3, denominator =2N2+3N3. Yes matches.\n\nNow evaluate for ratio B2:B3 =9:1. Let total B2 =9k, B3 = k. Then numerator =2*(9k)+6*k =18k+6k=24k. Denominator =2*9k+3*k =18k+3k=21k. So g_B =24/21 =8/7 \u22481.142857.\n\nExplain why simple molecular-number average functionality (i.e., weighted average f = (2*9+3*1)/10 = (18+3)/10=2.1) is not correct: Because selection is biased toward monomers with more functional groups; probability proportional to f_i, not equal per monomer. So need weighting by f_i.\n\nPart (2): Derive gel condition p_A p_B g_B =1. Since p_A = p_B = p, condition becomes p^2 g_B =1 => p_c = 1/\u221ag_B. With g_B =8/7, then \u221ag_B = sqrt(8/7)= sqrt(1.142857)=1.0690. So p_c = 1/1.0690 =0.9354 approx. Or compute directly: p_c = 1/\u221a(8/7) = sqrt(7/8) \u22480.935414.\n\nPart (3): We have 9.00 mol B2 and 1.00 mol B3. Total B functional groups =2*9 +3*1 =18+3=21 mol. Therefore need same number of A functional groups: each A2 provides 2 A groups per molecule. Number of A2 molecules needed = total A groups /2 =21/2 =10.5 mol. So require 10.5 mol A2.\n\nAt p=0.900 (< p_c ~0.935), system below gel point. Need X_n = initial number of monomer molecules / number of connected clusters. Initial total monomer molecules = N_total_initial = N_A2 + N_B2 + N_B3 = 10.5 + 9 + 1 =20.5 mol (but treat as molecules count). After reaction, some molecules combine forming clusters. In Flory-Stockmayer tree approximation without cycles, number of clusters equals number of \"originating\" monomers that start chains. Each chain starts from a monomer whose first bond formed? Actually each cluster corresponds to a \"root\" monomer that hasn't been attached to another monomer before? In tree growth, each new bond connects two fragments; each bond reduces number of clusters by 1. Initially each monomer is its own cluster: number of clusters = N_initial =20.5. Each reaction consumes one A-B pair, reducing clusters by 1. Number of reacted pairs = p * (total possible bonds?) At conversion p, fraction p of functional groups have reacted. Total possible bonds = total number of functional groups /2? Actually each bond consumes one A and one B functional group. Initial number of potential bonds = total A groups = total B groups =21. At conversion p, number of bonds formed = p * 21. So reduction in cluster number = p*21. Thus remaining clusters = N_initial - p*21. However need to account that after gelation, infinite cluster appears but still counts as one cluster. So X_n = N_initial / (N_initial - p*21). Plug numbers: N_initial =20.5, p=0.9, p*21=18.9. Clusters =20.5-18.9=1.6. X_n =20.5/1.6 =12.8125 \u224812.8. Check plausibility: X_n >1.\n\nAlternatively maybe they want number of connected clusters after polymerization including infinite cluster counted as one. So fine.\n\nPart (4): Need to derive extinction probability equations. Provide explanation: For a randomly chosen A functional group, probability that following it leads to infinite gel is something else. Extinction probability x_A is probability that chain dies out (does not reach infinite gel). Given p conversion, with probability (1-p) the functional group remains unreacted -> termination, so contributes to extinction. With probability p it reacts, connecting to a B functional group. That B functional group belongs to either B2 or B3 monomer with probabilities weighted by their functional group fractions: proportion of B groups belonging to B2 = (2*N2)/(total B groups) = (2*9)/(21)=18/21=6/7. To B3 = (3*N3)/21 =3/21=1/7. After reacting, the chain continues from the B monomer; the B monomer may have further unreacted B groups: for B2, after one bond used, remaining B groups =1 (so probability of extinction from that branch is x_B). For B3, after one bond used, remaining B groups =2, giving branching factor; the probability that both subsequent branches die out is x_B^2 (since independent). Hence equation: x_A = (1-p) + p[ (6/7)x_B + (1/7)x_B^2 ].\n\nSimilarly for B functional group: with prob (1-p) it's unreacted -> extinction (no continuation). With prob p it reacts to an A group; after reaction, the chain continues from A monomer; the A monomer is A2, having one remaining A group (since difunctional). So extinction probability from that side is x_A. Thus x_B = (1-p) + p x_A.\n\nNow solve for p=0.96. Compute numerically.\n\nLet p=0.96.\n\nEquation (2): x_B = 0.04 + 0.96 x_A.\n\nPlug into (1):\n\nx_A = 0.04 + 0.96[ (6/7) x_B + (1/7) x_B^2 ].\n\nSubstitute x_B.\n\nCompute using algebra.\n\nLet\u2019s do symbolic.\n\nLet\u2019s compute using Python mental? We'll do manual.\n\nLet x_B = 0.04 + 0.96 x_A.\n\nThen term inside brackets: (6/7)x_B + (1/7)x_B^2 = (1/7)(6 x_B + x_B^2).\n\nThus x_A = 0.04 + 0.96 * (1/7)(6 x_B + x_B^2) = 0.04 + (0.96/7)(6 x_B + x_B^2).\n\n0.96/7 = 0.137142857...\n\nSo x_A = 0.04 + 0.137142857*(6 x_B + x_B^2).\n\nNow substitute x_B expression.\n\nLet\u2019s denote a = 0.04, b = 0.96.\n\nx_B = a + b x_A.\n\nCompute 6 x_B + x_B^2 = 6(a + b x_A) + (a + b x_A)^2.\n\nExpand: =6a +6b x_A + a^2 +2ab x_A + b^2 x_A^2.\n\nCombine terms linear in x_A: (6b +2ab) x_A = 2a(3b + b?) wait compute: 6b +2ab = 2a b +6b = b(2a+6). Keep.\n\nConstant term: 6a + a^2.\n\nQuadratic term: b^2 x_A^2.\n\nThus x_A = a + (0.96/7)[ (6a + a^2) + b(2a+6) x_A + b^2 x_A^2 ].\n\nNow plug numbers: a=0.04, b=0.96.\n\nCompute constants:\n\n6a = 0.24.\n\na^2 = 0.0016.\n\nSum const C = 0.2416.\n\nCompute b(2a+6) = 0.96*(0.08+6) =0.96*6.08 =5.8368.\n\nb^2 = 0.9216.\n\nNow factor (0.96/7) = 0.137142857.\n\nThus x_A = 0.04 + 0.137142857*C + 0.137142857*5.8368 x_A + 0.137142857*0.9216 x_A^2.\n\nCompute 0.137142857*C: C=0.2416 \u2192 product =0.033142857? Let's calculate: 0.2416*0.137142857 = 0.033142857 (makes sense because 0.2416/7 =0.034514? Wait 0.2416/7 =0.034514, times 0.96? Actually 0.137142857 = 0.96/7. Multiply: 0.2416*0.96/7 = (0.231...?). Let's compute precisely: 0.2416*0.96 =0.231... 0.2416*0.96 =0.231... compute: 0.24*0.96=0.2304, plus 0.0016*0.96=0.001536, sum=0.231936. Divide by 7 =>0.033133714. Good close to 0.03313.\n\nNext coefficient linear: 0.137142857*5.8368 = (0.96/7)*5.8368 = (5.8368*0.96)/7 =5.604512/7 =0.80064457.\n\nQuadratic coeff: 0.137142857*0.9216 = (0.96/7)*0.9216 =0.884736/7 =0.12639086.\n\nThus equation:\n\nx_A = 0.04 + 0.0331337 + 0.800645 x_A + 0.126391 x_A^2.\n\nCombine constants: 0.0731337.\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n0 = 0.0731337 + 0.800645 x_A + 0.126391 x_A^2 - x_A\n\n=> 0 = 0.0731337 + (0.800645 -1) x_A + 0.126391 x_A^2\n\n=> 0 = 0.0731337 -0.199355 x_A + 0.126391 x_A^2.\n\nMultiply by 100000 maybe. Solve quadratic: 0.126391 x_A^2 -0.199355 x_A +0.0731337 =0.\n\nUse formula: discriminant D = (-0.199355)^2 -4*0.126391*0.0731337.\n\nCompute squares: 0.199355^2 =0.039749? Let's compute: 0.199355*0.199355 \u22480.039749 (approx). Now compute product: 4*0.126391*0.0731337 = 4*0.009222? Let's compute 0.126391*0.0731337 = 0.009224? Multiply: 0.126391*0.07=0.00884737; plus 0.126391*0.0031337\u22480.000396; sum\u22480.009243. Times 4 =>0.036972. So D \u22480.039749 -0.036972 =0.002777.\n\nSquare root sqrt(D) \u22480.05270.\n\nSolutions: x_A = [0.199355 \u00b1 0.05270]/(2*0.126391)= [0.199355 \u00b10.05270]/0.252782.\n\nTwo possibilities:\n\n1) plus: (0.252055)/0.252782 =0.9970 approx.\n\n2) minus: (0.146655)/0.252782 =0.5804.\n\nPhysical solution should be <1, both less than 1. But which corresponds to extinction probability? Should be less than 1 but also small? Typically extinction probability near 0 when conversion high. Here p=0.96 > p_c, we expect some finite probability of being in sol (extinction) less than 1. Both 0.997 and 0.58 are <1. However the larger one corresponds to almost certain extinction (0.997) meaning chain rarely reaches infinite gel, contradictory to being above gel point where most chains join infinite gel. So choose smaller value 0.580. So x_A \u22480.58, x_B = 0.04 +0.96*0.58 =0.04+0.5568=0.5968 \u22480.597.\n\nCheck consistency: x_B should be slightly higher than x_A due to extra branching; indeed 0.597 vs 0.58.\n\nThus nontrivial solution is lower root.\n\nPart (5): Fraction of monomer molecules belonging to infinite gel. For each monomer type, probability it ends up in sol (not in infinite gel) is product of extinction probabilities of its branches.\n\nFor A2: both A groups must be extinct (each branch independent) => probability sol = x_A^2. For B2: both B groups must be extinct => sol prob = x_B^2. For B3: all three B groups must be extinct => sol prob = x_B^3.\n\nOverall fraction in sol = (N_A2 * x_A^2 + N_B2 * x_B^2 + N_B3 * x_B^3) / total monomers (N_total =20.5). Then fraction in infinite gel = 1 - that.\n\nPlug numbers: N_A2 =10.5, N_B2=9, N_B3=1.\n\nCompute x_A^2 =0.580^2 =0.3364. x_B^2 =0.597^2 =0.3564. x_B^3 =0.597^3 =0.215? Compute: 0.597^2=0.3564, multiply by 0.597 =>0.212? Let's compute: 0.3564*0.597 \u22480.212. More precise: 0.3564*0.6=0.21384 minus 0.3564*0.003=0.001069 =>0.21277. Good.\n\nNow contributions: A2 contribution =10.5*0.3364=3.5322. B2 contribution =9*0.3564=3.2076. B3 contribution =1*0.2128=0.2128. Sum sol monomers =7.0 (approx). Divide by total 20.5 => fraction sol =0.341. Therefore fraction in infinite gel =0.659.\n\nThus about 66% of monomer molecules are in the infinite gel.\n\nPart (6): Explanation: The trivial solution x_A=x_B=1 corresponds to the case where every chain inevitably reaches the infinite gel, i.e., extinction probability zero. This occurs only when the branching factor exceeds unity at zero conversion (p=0) which is not our situation. Above gel point, the branching process has a positive probability of survival (joining infinite gel) but also a non-zero probability of extinction (remaining in sol). The nontrivial root (<1) represents the true extinction probability; the unit solution is mathematically admissible but physically irrelevant because it would imply zero chance of being in sol, contradicting the existence of finite clusters observed experimentally. Stability analysis: For p>p_c, the generating function of the branching process has two fixed points: one stable (nontrivial extinction probability) and one unstable (unit). The stable fixed point governs long-time behavior. The gel-point criterion derived via p_c = 1/\u221ag_B ensures that the mean progeny >1, leading to a supercritical branching process with a nontrivial extinction probability less than 1. The unit solution corresponds to the boundary between subcritical (p<p_c) where extinction probability =1, and supercritical (p>p_c) where extinction probability <1. Hence the nontrivial solution is selected.\n\nNow craft final answer with clear steps, formulas, numeric results, explanations."
